= Public health and forensic medicine bibliography

= Nuisances Removal Act for England (Amendment) Act 1863
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=26 & 27 Vict. c.117}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/26-27/117/pdfs/ukpga_18630117_en.pdf

Original printed Act, enacted 28 July 1863. Section 2 provides inspection, seizure and a justice's disposal order for unfit food; it does not provide an exhaustive disease-by-disease whole-carcass list.

= Current United States meat-inspection regulations, 9 CFR Part 311
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311

Present United States comparative criteria, specifically https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311/section-311.2[§311.2, tuberculosis] and https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311/section-311.16[§311.16, carcasses presenting a food-poisoning risk]. These are not English law in 1864.

= Lead in Drinking Water by the Drinking Water Inspectorate
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.dwi.gov.uk/lead-in-drinking-water/

“Where does lead come from?” and “How does lead get into drinking water?”: plumbing, stagnation, temperature, <water hardness>, disturbance and intermittent particles.

= Pipe and Fittings Corrosion by the Drinking Water Inspectorate
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.dwi.gov.uk/pipe-and-fittings-corrosion-2/

Present institutional explanation of <lead> solubility, <pH>, <alkalinity> and deposits; used for chemistry, not a period legal limit.

= Heat-related Illnesses by CDC and NIOSH
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/heat-stress/about/illnesses.html

Heatstroke, emergency response and rapid cooling; current page dated March 2026.

= Health of the Navy: Home Station report (1864)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.pdavis.nl/H1864_Home.htm

Transcription of the official 1864 naval medical report, venereal-disease discussion. It distinguishes infecting sores, soft sores, buboes, relapses, mercurial practice and disagreement. The transcription is not a newly collated facsimile.

= Health Tract No.154: Disinfectants (1865)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1865}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9199822/

1865, volume 12, p.154. Primary tract on cleanliness, deodorization and proposed disinfectants. Its recipes, miasma explanations and oxygen-refreshing claims are not endorsed.

= Water, Sanitation and Health in Humanitarian Emergencies by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/teams/environment-climate-change-and-health/water-sanitation-and-health/environmental-health-in-emergencies/humanitarian-emergencies

Institutional explanation of epidemic risks, enteric disease, contact exposures and mosquito breeding. Cited to distinguish exposure routes, not to prescribe a historical water allowance.

= The British Soldier in India in The British and Foreign Medico-Chirurgical Review (1861)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1861}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5182393/

January 1861, 27(53), pp.38–48; especially pp.43–46, an asserted lime-water explanation for goitre and cretinism. This primary period review documents the historical belief, not a correct modern causal conclusion. PMC metadata misspells “Soldier” as “Solider”.

= Drinking-water by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/drinking-water

Current institutional fact sheet: enteric infections, chemically contaminated water and lead from water-system components. “Water and health” separates several mechanisms.

= On the Mode of Communication of Cholera by John Snow
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1855}
{tag=Work by John Snow}

https://epi-snow.ph.ucla.edu/Stream3_GrandExperiment_a.html

Second edition, 1855, pp.55–98, institutional transcription of the water-supply comparison. Corroborating complete transcription: https://www.academia.dk/MedHist/Biblioteket/Print/snow_1855.html.

= Measuring Mechanical Ventilation Airflow by Pacific Northwest National Laboratory
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://basc.pnnl.gov/resource-guides/measuring-mechanical-ventilation-airflow

Institutional technical guide, “How to Calculate Airflow Rate from a Velocity Probe Measurement”: cross-sectional averaging and anemometer limitations. Used as a modern comparison rather than a historical instrument specification.

= Air Change Rate: Input Parameter Protocol by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2020}

https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/air-change-rate-input-parameter-protocol

2020 HOMES/PT model input protocol. Source cessation, tracer concentration decay, background conditions and interpretation; a later measurement comparison.

= Is the Lung Floating Test a Valuable Tool or Obsolete? A Prospective Autopsy Study
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2013}

https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22733108/

International Journal of Legal Medicine 127(2), 2013, pp.447–451; doi:10.1007/s00414-012-0727-1. Primary prospective study abstract: four false negatives despite reported prior life. No universal accuracy guarantee is inferred.

= Forensic Examinations of Newborns 1910–1912
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2013}

https://tidsskriftet.no/en/2013/12/child-has-lived-and-breathed-forensic-examinations-newborns-1910-1912-0

Original case-record investigation published in 2013, pp.2498–2501, doi:10.4045/tidsskr.13.0898. Full title begins “The child has lived and breathed.” Discusses historical theory, decomposition and actual use of flotation tests.

= Permit-required Confined Spaces by the Occupational Safety and Health Administration
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.146

Present US regulation 29 CFR 1910.146, cited solely as a technical comparison for atmosphere testing, ventilation and rescue. Not represented as English law in 1865.

= Carbon Dioxide by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npg/npgd0103.html

Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards: properties, gas density, symptoms and target organs. No background-atmosphere value or occupational numerical limit is imported into the historical answer.

= Septic System Impacts on Water Sources by United States Environmental Protection Agency
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

Institutional account of groundwater and surface-water contamination, including nutrients and pathogens. Cited sections: Protect Your Drinking Water Well, Protect Your Local Surface Water and frequent questions on water quality.

https://www.epa.gov/septic/septic-system-impacts-water-sources

= Common Considerations for Source Water Protection by United States Environmental Protection Agency
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

Institutional source-water guidance. Cited Contaminants That Can Affect Drinking Water, Nutrients: sources of nitrate. The solution uses the source account, not the page's modern legal limit.

https://www.epa.gov/sourcewaterprotection/common-considerations

= About Lead in Drinking Water by CDC
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

Institutional guidance from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Cited Overview and Exposure sections: plumbing sources, corrosion, water chemistry and contact conditions. No modern regulatory provision is used as an 1865 rule.

https://www.cdc.gov/lead-prevention/prevention/drinking-water.html

= Hardness in Drinking-water by World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

Institutional chemical fact sheet. Cited passage: Chemical Fact Sheets, hardness, printed p. 377, naturally low mineral content of rainwater and the soap-reaction description. Used to corroborate chemistry and qualify the historical generalization, not to supply a numerical health threshold.

https://www.who.int/docs/default-source/wash-documents/wash-chemicals/hardness-chemical-fact-sheet.pdf

= Sanitary Science and Legislation in The British and Foreign Medico-Chirurgical Review (1865)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1865}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5180500/

January 1865, pp.37–38 in review of the 1860–1863 Medical Officer reports. Analytical disagreement, phosphate interference and reagent contamination in alum determination.

= Food and Its Adulterations by Arthur Hill Hassall
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1855}
{tag=Work by Arthur Hill Hassall}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Medical_Heritage_Library_%28IA_b2135554x%29.pdf

London, Longman, Brown, Green and Longmans, 1855. Introduction and “Bread, and its Adulterations”, including p.143: observed samples must be distinguished from general allegations.

= Environmental Sampling by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/environmental-control/environmental-sampling.html

Environmental infection-control guideline, Air sampling. Method-dependent results, comparison conditions, limits on interpretation and settling plates.

= Particulates Not Otherwise Regulated, Total, Method 0500 by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1994}

https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/docs/2003-154/pdfs/0500.pdf

NIOSH method 0500, Issue 2, 15 August 1994. Modern gravimetric measurement, controlled filter handling and field blanks; no claim that its specified occupational range detects all hospital bioaerosols.

= Ammonia by Ion Chromatography, Method 6016 by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/docs/2014-151/pdfs/methods/6016.pdf

NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, fifth edition, method 6016. Modern acid-treated sorbent and ion-chromatographic analytical comparison.

= Cholera Outbreaks by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cholera-outbreaks

Current household care guidance includes laundering, boiling or chlorine options, drying, handwashing and protection of drinking-water sources.

= Instructions on Disinfection in Choleraic Diseases (1866)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1866}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5867774/

Glasgow Medical Journal 1(6), October 1866, pp.244–246. Primary instructions on soiled linen, discharge contamination and period disinfectants; alkaline-decomposition theory explicitly distinguished from current knowledge.

= Third Report of the Cattle Plague Commissioners (1866)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1866}

https://www.queensu.ca/academia/forsdyke/rindpst2.htm

Transcribed primary 1866 report. Discussion of tar acids and sulphurous acid is historical evidence of contemporary evaluation; animal-disease experiments are not treated as proof for every human infection.

= Chemical Disinfectants by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/disinfection-sterilization/chemical-disinfectants.html

Guideline for Disinfection and Sterilization in Healthcare Facilities: chlorine compounds and phenolics, mechanisms and limitations.

= Disaster Victim Identification by INTERPOL
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.interpol.int/How-we-work/Forensics/Disaster-Victim-Identification-DVI

Institutional identification method: post-mortem and ante-mortem records, dental and genetic identification, and reconciliation.

= Skeleton Secrets by Arizona State University
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://askanexpert.asu.edu/anthropology/listen-watch/skeleton-secrets

Institutional expert interview: skeletal age, adult pelvic morphology and long-bone estimation of stature.

= Forensic Anthropology by the Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://naturalhistory.si.edu/education/teaching-resources/anthropology-and-social-studies/forensic-anthropology

Institutional explanation of skeletal growth, sex estimation, pathology and comparative collections.

= Caring for Women Subjected to Violence by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/hrp/vaw/slide-deck.pdf?sfvrsn=6253f4a7_7

Training curriculum, Session 11, documentation of sexual assault: consent, objective injury descriptions and the limitations of absent physical evidence.

= Eliminating Virginity Testing by the World Health Organization and Partner Agencies
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2018}

https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/WHO-RHR-18.15

16 October 2018, interagency statement WHO/RHR/18.15. No examination can establish sexual history from hymenal appearance or vaginal laxity.

= Guidelines for Medico-legal Care for Victims of Sexual Violence by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2003}

https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/42788/924154628X.pdf?sequence=1

2003, chapter 4, especially sections 4.5–4.6, examination, injuries and specimen collection. Historical legal elements are supported separately by the original 1861 statute.

= Offences against the Person Act 1861
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1861}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/24-25/100/pdfs/ukpga_18610100_en.pdf

24 & 25 Vict. c.100, original enacted text, 6 August 1861. Section 48: rape a felony; section 63: penetration sufficient for carnal knowledge without emission.

= Ambient outdoor air pollution by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ambient-(outdoor)-air-quality-and-health

Fact sheet; overview and health effects of inhaled pollutants.

= Sanitation by the World Health Organization
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/sanitation

Fact sheet; “Sanitation and health” and “WHO response”.

= The Sewage Question in The Spectator (6 July 1867)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://archive.spectator.co.uk/article/6th-july-1867/23/the-sewage-question-by-frederick-charles-kropp-lon

Contemporary review, p.23, describing competing drainage and collection proposals and the risk of operational failure.

= Sewage Irrigation at the Cumberland Asylum
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://doi.org/10.1192/bjp.11.56.609

Journal of Mental Science 11(56), January 1866, pp.609–610. Contemporary evidence that the practice was being discussed before the Oxford examination; not proof of universal safety.

= Legal medicine by Charles Meymott Tidy (volume 3)
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Charles Meymott Tidy}
{title2=1884}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Legal_medicine_%28IA_legalmedicine03tidy%29.pdf

New York: William Wood, 1884. Pages 59–60 record the 294-day interval and case 112 (1834). This is retrospective evidence about the earlier decision, not a source to substitute for contemporary 1867 statutes.

= Medical Jurisprudence in the eighth edition of Encyclopædia Britannica
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://jimclifford.ca/britannica-articles-4/a/EB.16/193696088/407.html

Digitized transcription of the 1853–1860 eighth edition, with a link to the National Library of Scotland scan. Part I, Section I, VI.3 states that English law has no definite upper limit for <gestation>.

= Wound Vitality in Decomposed Bodies: New Frontiers Through Immunohistochemistry
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2021.802841/full

Original research, Frontiers in Medicine 8:802841, published 24 December 2021. A pilot study of <immunohistochemistry> in decomposed forensic material; the authors describe its conclusions as inconclusive and advocate combined markers.

= Lunacy Act 1890
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/53-54/5/contents

53 & 54 Vict. c.5. Later legislation, not the law to substitute for admission requirements in 1867.

= Lunacy Acts Amendment Act 1862
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/25-26/111/pdfs/ukpga_18620111_en.pdf

25 & 26 Vict. c.111, enacted 7 August 1862. Original printed statute; especially §§22–25 and 28.

= Lunatic Asylums Act 1853
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/16-17/97/pdfs/ukpga_18530097_en.pdf

16 & 17 Vict. c.97. Original printed statute; especially §§67–74 and Schedule F.

= Lunatics Care and Treatment Act 1853
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/16-17/96/pdfs/ukpga_18530096_en.pdf

16 & 17 Vict. c.96, enacted 20 August 1853. Original printed statute; especially §§4–5, 10, 12–13 and Schedule A.

= Lunacy Act 1845
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/8-9/100/contents

8 & 9 Vict. c.100. Read with the 1853 and 1862 amendments when examining 1867 practice.

= Confirmatory blood tests by the National Institute of Justice
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://nij.ojp.gov/nij-hosted-online-training-courses/laboratory-orientation-and-testing-body-fluids-and-tissues/testing-body-fluids-tissues/blood/confirmatory-tests

Official training page distinguishing crystal confirmation of <blood> from human-origin testing using antisera.

= Identification of blood by the National Institute of Justice
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}

https://www.ojp.gov/pdffiles1/pr/160880_unit_2.pdf

Unit II of Sourcebook in Forensic Serology, Immunology, and Biochemistry. §§3–6 cover historical identification, microscopic methods, crystals and spectral methods.

= Escherichia coli in the Australian Drinking Water Guidelines
{parent=Public health and forensic medicine bibliography}
{c}
{title2=endorsed 2011; current institutional page inspected}

Institutional guidance by the National Health and Medical Research Council. Cited sections: Guideline, General description, Indicator value and application in practice. These concern indicator specificity, the 100 mL sample criterion and limits concerning protozoa and viruses.

https://guidelines.nhmrc.gov.au/australian-drinking-water-guidelines/part-5/microorganisms/microbial-indicators/escherichia-coli
